# Upstream Update — Token-Light Rebase

When asked to update this repo from upstream (dramaclaw/dramaclaw) **and local patches exist**:

## Rules

1. **Always `fetch` + `rebase`, never `merge`** — rebase replays local patches on top of upstream.
   ```bash
   git fetch upstream --quiet || git remote add upstream https://github.com/dramaclaw/dramaclaw && git fetch upstream --quiet
   git rebase upstream/main
   ```
2. **Never dump git output into chat.** Redirect to a file, then read only the summary:
   ```bash
   git rebase upstream/main > /tmp/rb.log 2>&1
   grep -c CONFLICT /tmp/rb.log   # how many conflicts
   grep -n '^UU' /tmp/rb.log      # only conflicting files
   ```
3. **Working tree must be clean first.** If `git status --short` shows changes: `git stash push -m "wip"` (or commit) before rebasing.
4. **Resolve conflicts manually**, then `git add <files> && git rebase --continue`.
5. **To bail out**: `git rebase --abort`.

## Why
A plain `git merge upstream/main` prints a 200+ file listing that stays in context and burns tokens. Redirect + summarize instead.
